WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.870
- Every nation should
be able to set it's own

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.850
laws and it's own customs
and it's own traditions

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:08.030
and all of those things.

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:10.360
And that's going to be different
than every other country.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.680
Now that doesn't mean that
one is necessarily worse

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.810
or one is necessarily better.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.540
But most likely every nation
is gonna do something different

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.447
and that strong nations
first at the bottom

00:00:20.447 --> 00:00:22.860
and then what can build a sort of

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.370
world wide system that we can all play by.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:26.870
And what we've done backwards,

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:28.853
and this is when people
talk about globalism,

00:00:28.853 --> 00:00:32.210
is we think that oh if
we could just be governed

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.330
by one thing first

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:35.620
then we can sort of set

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.650
that down to all of the individual states.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:41.134
And that, I just don't buy into that idea?

00:00:41.134 --> 00:00:43.967
(energetic music)

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.235
- Hi everybody, Michael Shermer
here talking to Dave Rubin

00:00:52.235 --> 00:00:55.610
about his new book,
"Don't Burn This Book."

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.410
I tried to burn it last night 'cause I ran

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:59.780
out of firewood and it just wouldn't take.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.120
So I don't know what the deal is here.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.162
They got special paper or something.

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:06.765
- We sent you the fire
retardant copy because you're a

00:01:06.765 --> 00:01:09.463
skeptic and I knew you'd be up to no good.

00:01:11.288 --> 00:01:12.438
- That's right.

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.181
Sort of like polyester
suits, they don't burn.

00:01:14.181 --> 00:01:17.900
So, well I read your whole book.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.750
I did it on audio and
read it before we talked

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.535
before on my podcast
From the Print Edition.

00:01:24.535 --> 00:01:26.790
So congratulations on the book.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.541
I thought we'd just hit some of the key

00:01:29.541 --> 00:01:31.668
points in chapter three
because to me I think

00:01:31.668 --> 00:01:33.060
this is where the rubber meets the road.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.710
It's one thing to say, "Well,
I'm in favor of freedom."

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:37.604
Everybody's in favor of
freedom if you ask them.

00:01:37.604 --> 00:01:41.510
But what about this point,
or what about that point

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:42.810
is kind of where it mattered.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.600
Now, you and I are
largely on the same page.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.890
I think I'll channel my inner progressive

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:51.778
far left person I see on
cable news and just kind of

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:56.778
try to steelman the other side.

00:01:57.159 --> 00:01:57.992
- Great.

00:01:58.903 --> 00:02:01.100
- So we'll get the major
issues here in chapter three,

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.360
drugs, immigration,
foreign policy, economics,

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.063
and starting with drugs.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.280
Well, so you fess up
having done a few yourself.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.467
So, I guess--

00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:13.590
- I've done most of the good ones.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.007
- I guess somebody could
accuse you of being

00:02:16.007 --> 00:02:18.750
in favor of the decriminalization of drugs

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:19.620
for personal reasons.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.770
But of course you're
not arguing that here.

00:02:23.037 --> 00:02:25.522
You're arguing for in
terms of personal liberty.

00:02:25.522 --> 00:02:29.350
I guess it would apply something
like the harm principle.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:33.060
As long as you're not harming
anybody what's the problem?

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.580
Well, the problem I would
guess that conservatives might

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:41.820
argue is it does harm people indirectly.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:46.650
So let's say, you know, it
drives insurance costs up

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.930
because you personally have
to be treated medically or

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:52.710
whatever and the rest of us
have to pay for that through

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.030
our premiums.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.128
Or worse, it would be
something like the broken

00:02:57.128 --> 00:02:59.230
windows theory of crime
where a little things

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.070
that bring the neighborhood
down a little bit

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.340
drug dealers on the corner or whatever,

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:06.390
you know, why don't we just
let them deal drugs like the

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:07.740
liquor store sells liquor,

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.200
the gas station sells cigarettes.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.820
The counter argument to
that I think would be,

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.350
well, but it brings down the neighborhood

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:17.630
like graffiti on walls sends a signal

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:20.210
to everybody this
neighborhood is not being

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:21.440
monitored by the police.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.280
We don't give a crap about standards here.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.113
Do what you want.

00:03:25.113 --> 00:03:27.300
And that leads to petty
crimes and then that

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.050
escalates to major crimes
and before you know it

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:31.940
you have a run away homicide effect

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.490
like in the 70s in New York City.

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.371
So what's your response to that?

00:03:36.371 --> 00:03:38.620
- So that's why I love you
Shermer because you went

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.760
out of your way to steelman
some of the oppositional stuff.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.290
So, my basic feelings
about this are first off,

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:48.203
and I make this point, if
you're for legalized alcohol,

00:03:48.203 --> 00:03:50.810
which pretty much every is,

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.320
then you really can't make an argument

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:55.870
at least against legalizing marijuana.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.840
I mean the amount of medical problems

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.980
and most of the domestic
violence and all of the

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.830
negative things, drunk driving that come

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.870
with alcohol there's
actually no evidence that any

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.380
of those things come with marijuana.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:09.710
So that's one.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.110
And then I would expand,

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.220
but by the way I'm completely okay leaving

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:15.740
it to the states, which is
how we're doing it right now.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.100
It was first medically
available in certain states

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.810
and now recreationally available,

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:21.210
and I'm completely fine with that.

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.530
And if you don't want it
in your state you can move

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:24.363
to another state.

00:04:24.363 --> 00:04:26.650
And what the states have found actually is

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:30.560
that when they recreationally allowed it

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.530
not only are they not
really seeing a major

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.030
increase in marijuana usage,

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:36.100
well then they're also
generating tax revenue.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.830
Colorado is probably the
best example of this.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:43.830
I'm also okay generally with
legalizing some other drugs,

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.180
so like so psilocybin, magic mushrooms,

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.530
things like that.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.490
Then where I get into
the broken windows part,

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.292
which I think is completely legit

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:54.920
is that when I talk
about the light touch of

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.770
government and things from
a classical or libertarian

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:00.948
perspective and I have a lot
of libertarians that have

00:05:00.948 --> 00:05:02.560
been reading this already
that are kind of pissed at me.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:04.360
And my friend Michael
Malice I went on his show

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:07.710
and we duked it out all in good spirit.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.490
He wants to legalize everything.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.180
My feeling is where
this is where government

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.980
has to come in and do some stuff.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:14.880
First off, I don't want
to put people in jail

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.140
for doing drugs.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:17.772
I simply don't want to do that.

00:05:17.772 --> 00:05:20.800
And I think there's
better ways either through

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.180
rehabilitation and hopefully
most of that can even

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.250
be done privately, but I
would be willing to put

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:29.060
some public funds to
getting people off drugs.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:31.820
But I don't want to just jail
people because they've made

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:33.770
some, let's say, rough choices,

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:36.070
or negative personal choices.

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.118
That being said I also admit
to doing a lot of stuff

00:05:38.118 --> 00:05:40.330
back in the day, and should
I have been put in jail

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:41.163
had I been caught.

00:05:41.163 --> 00:05:44.120
And we also know there's
socioeconomic reasons that certain

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.830
types of people will be
caught more frequently

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.600
than other types of
people and a whole bunch

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:49.600
of other stuff.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:53.880
But what I would say in short
is you have to have some rule.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.330
So the simple answer on this is look,

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:59.060
if my neighbor, if we legalized everything

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.300
and then I found out that
my neighbor was making meth

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:05.660
in his kitchen or it
turned into a crack den

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:08.250
or something like that not
only do we know that there

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:11.010
would be all sorts of
nefarious people coming around,

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.116
that there would be more crime,

00:06:13.116 --> 00:06:14.447
it would be less safe
for kids to come around,

00:06:14.447 --> 00:06:16.280
and a whole slue of things.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:20.210
Now, specifically making
everything illegal or legal

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:23.130
doesn't necessarily take
of all of those problems.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:25.250
But I, this is just one of those places

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:28.760
where I feel you just need
some guidelines on society.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.563
And where I guess the rich conversation

00:06:31.563 --> 00:06:33.273
that we could have,
and we sort of did this

00:06:33.273 --> 00:06:34.489
when you had me on your podcast,

00:06:34.489 --> 00:06:35.390
we could whittle this down to okay,

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:38.080
then what drugs exactly
are we talking about

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:38.950
on all of these things.

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:42.870
So is it marijuana and say
medicinal mushrooms and

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:47.240
ayahuasca, sort of the psychedelic stuff

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.460
that generally people do
when there isn't like a

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:53.100
hardcore addiction, and is
the hardcore addiction heroin,

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.170
meth, things like that.

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:55.530
Is that the line?

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.550
It's a great place to
have that back and forth,

00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:58.700
but what I was trying to do here,

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:00.190
which is what I try to
do with the whole book

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:03.840
is just give some basic
guidelines and then let's sort of

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.670
whittle down the differences.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.080
- Yeah, you talked about
the taxation of cigarettes

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:09.670
in New York City.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.080
I had forgotten how
expensive it is to smoke

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.686
because I've never smoked.

00:07:13.686 --> 00:07:16.040
- It's crazy in New York City, yeah.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:18.680
- New York state tax on
a pack of 20 cigarettes

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:19.865
is $4.35.

00:07:19.865 --> 00:07:22.750
20 cigarettes, there's people
that do that every day.

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:27.610
While New York City tax
$1.50 comprises $5.85

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:28.919
of the $14 total.

00:07:28.919 --> 00:07:31.630
New York is now the black market capital

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:34.758
in the United States for
illegal cigarette sales.

00:07:34.758 --> 00:07:36.363
So that I presume is
just to avoid the taxes.

00:07:36.363 --> 00:07:38.713
So there's a problem with--

00:07:38.713 --> 00:07:40.770
- Well, it's also that, you know,

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:43.590
if you're taxing people
on vices in a weird way

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:46.570
as the state you end up
needing people to have vices

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:47.620
to generate revenue.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:49.730
And I don't like that idea
that we're gonna sort of

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:53.610
in a weird way need the very
people we're trying to stop

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:54.880
from doing these things.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.130
Again, this is where I just
wanna get the government

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:58.740
out of the way as much as possible.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.277
But I don't want a crack den next door.

00:08:00.277 --> 00:08:02.690
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:04.238
Woody Allen had a funny bit about

00:08:04.238 --> 00:08:07.050
to get divorced in New York state,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:09.951
this is back in the 60s,
there had to be a cause.

00:08:09.951 --> 00:08:14.320
So as he said, what was it,

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.357
adultery is required in
New York City by law.

00:08:19.397 --> 00:08:20.230
- Exactly, so you have to have it.

00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:21.950
- You have to have it yeah.

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:23.840
So anyway, that's kind of funny.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.420
Yeah, so that's the
problem, well so the counter

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:29.503
argument to legalizing
pot say and then taxing it

00:08:29.503 --> 00:08:32.923
is that if you drive the
taxes too high or the

00:08:32.923 --> 00:08:37.923
regulatory bureaucracy
to set up a pot farm

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.240
and so on is so large
that people then just do

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.040
it on the sly anyway to avoid all that.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.674
And I think there has
been some cases in Oregon

00:08:49.674 --> 00:08:52.030
where there's a lot of
legal pot growers still

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.420
because they can't get the license,

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:54.910
or they don't have the
money for the license

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:56.379
and then all the taxes.

00:08:56.379 --> 00:08:59.100
- Right, and all of that
I would leave up to then

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:01.300
hopefully some other people
come in and they find

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:03.045
cheaper ways to make marijuana,

00:09:03.045 --> 00:09:05.050
or to grow marijuana or everything else.

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:07.470
That's where competition
I would hope would

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:09.200
depress some of those costs.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:10.510
But again, it's not perfect.

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:12.510
And that's what I try to
say throughout the book.

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.960
You're dealing with these issues honestly

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:17.520
as opposed to sort of this
utopian idea that we can

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.390
create a perfect law for
everybody that's gonna stop

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:22.820
everyone from doing all
the bad things and they're

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:24.586
only gonna do the good things.

00:09:24.586 --> 00:09:26.070
That's just not real.

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:27.990
So what I try to do is get out of people's

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:29.540
way as much as possible,

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:33.460
but then also say, we've
got to add some guard rail

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:36.110
so that we're not completely
going off the deep end.

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:37.290
- Okay, gay marriage.

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.770
This ones kind of hard for
me to steelman the other side

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:43.220
because, you know, I'm so in favor of it.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:45.900
But we don't have to
go back very far, 2011,

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:49.520
both Hilary and Obama were
against it before that.

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:51.750
And pretty much that was the trend.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.040
So what were their arguments?

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:56.430
Okay, let's see, you know
the state sanctions marriage

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:59.700
for a reason because the
state wants citizens to have

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:03.000
more babies so marriage
is sort of the foundation

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:03.933
for family with children.

00:10:03.933 --> 00:10:07.480
And of course that is an ancient tradition

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:08.514
grounded in religion.

00:10:08.514 --> 00:10:10.820
But the state, you know,
gives you a tax break

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:12.570
for being married because they want

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.888
people to be married so
they have kids and so on,

00:10:14.888 --> 00:10:19.261
and that somehow allowing gay marriage,

00:10:19.261 --> 00:10:22.736
then what, people can
marry their pets, you know,

00:10:22.736 --> 00:10:26.000
or having polygamous families.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:27.700
'cause if you want lots of children let

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:29.838
guys marry 20 women and
have tons of children.

00:10:29.838 --> 00:10:33.080
You know, so I guess they
were making something of

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.400
a slippery slope argument
that if you allow

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:39.900
that then you put a crack
in the foundation of

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:43.190
why the state endorses
marriage in the first place.

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:45.423
- And then on top of that
there's just sort of the more

00:10:45.423 --> 00:10:48.270
typically conservative
argument, which is also

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:50.100
bound up in religion and the bible

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:52.790
and a man and a women and man

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:55.840
should not lie with another
man, and that sort of stuff.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:58.440
Which I know, certainly
know your feelings on that.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.856
I mean we don't have to
spend too much time on this

00:11:00.856 --> 00:11:02.990
because in many ways the ship
has sailed and the loudest

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:04.623
voices against gap marriage,

00:11:05.544 --> 00:11:07.865
I mean the Christian right,
you know when you think

00:11:07.865 --> 00:11:09.510
about the Mike Huckabees
and the Rick Santorums

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:12.549
they actually have completely let it go.

00:11:12.549 --> 00:11:14.177
Now that doesn't mean they have their,

00:11:14.177 --> 00:11:16.070
they don't have their
personal religious beliefs.

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:19.130
But as an issue I find that
most of the people that we know

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:21.370
like Ben Shapiro who has his own personal

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:24.450
religious belief he's taken
the libertarian approach,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.290
which is either that the state
shouldn't be involved at all

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:31.010
or it shouldn't be something,
you know, I can have my

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:32.070
beliefs and you can have yours.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:34.640
So I'm actually okay with that
and I'm thrilled that this

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.950
really is no longer an issue.

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:40.500
And I can just accept, and
this is where a lot of lefties

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:41.620
get angry at me.

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:44.920
But I can just accept that
some people may not be

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:46.620
happy with all my life choices.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:48.880
It's okay, I'm happy with them.

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:50.180
And that's enough for me.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.945
So I don't even think
it's really one that,

00:11:52.945 --> 00:11:54.810
I sort of wrote it very quickly
because it was kind of like

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:56.810
the ship has sailed and
you know when you get wins

00:11:56.810 --> 00:11:58.820
I think this is one of
the things that people

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:00.010
don't do well in politics.

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:02.710
When you get a win be gracious about it.

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:05.099
Like, I don't need to now
rail against and say oh,

00:12:05.099 --> 00:12:08.010
all of you used to be against it and keep

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:09.330
clubbing over the head.

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:10.770
Guys, we got a win.

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:11.603
Let's move on.

00:12:11.603 --> 00:12:13.090
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:15.190
Let's move on to a much
harder problem I think

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:16.731
is immigration.

00:12:16.731 --> 00:12:19.667
Obviously we don't want to open
the borders up to everybody,

00:12:19.667 --> 00:12:23.140
and we don't want to close the
borders so nobody comes in.

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:23.990
What's the right balance?

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:25.630
How do you find that?

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:28.220
- That was a very dangerous
word you used there my friend

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.120
obviously because you know a lot of people

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:31.250
don't find that obvious.

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:35.120
I mean there's sort of the
lefty version of open borders

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:36.900
and sort of no nation states.

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:40.357
And then there's the very
strong libertarian argument

00:12:40.357 --> 00:12:41.225
for that too.

00:12:41.225 --> 00:12:42.900
I am completely against
both of those arguments.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:44.890
That is an odd one for the
horseshoe theory, right,

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:47.250
because you've got the far
lefties and the libertarians

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:48.950
both saying just open it
up and we'll just have

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:50.930
one passport and you
can wonder through and

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:52.010
commerce and all that.

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:55.460
Look, I believe in strong nation states.

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:59.100
I believe in the American
ethos that is special,

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.030
that is different than a Canadian ethos

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:02.760
and is different than a Mexican ethos.

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:04.454
We are separate countries.

00:13:04.454 --> 00:13:06.697
The book that I reference in the chapter,

00:13:06.697 --> 00:13:09.040
"The Virtue of Nationalism"
by Yoram Hazony

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:12.290
his whole argument is that
every nation should be

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:16.730
able to set it's own
laws and it's own customs

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:18.710
and it's own traditions
and all of those things.

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:21.060
And that's going to be different
than every other country.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:24.370
Now that doesn't mean that
one is necessarily worse,

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:25.500
or one is necessarily better.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:28.230
But most likely every nation
is gonna do something different

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:31.810
and that strong nations
first at the bottom are then

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.930
what can build a sort of
world wide system that

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:36.060
we can all play by.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.320
And what we've done backwards,
and this is when people

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:41.360
talk about globalism is we think that oh,

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:45.010
if we could all just be
governed by one thing first

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:47.070
then we can sort of set that down

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:49.320
to all of the individual states.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.410
And that, I just don't buy into that idea.

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:53.420
I don't buy into it from
an American perspective

00:13:53.420 --> 00:13:54.780
when I talk about the federal government.

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:56.830
I like things to go this way.

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:58.160
And so that really is the argument.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:13:59.480
I want anything strong nation states.

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:03.470
I think every country from
the United States to Paraguay

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:05.530
has the right to set
whatever their immigration

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:08.130
policy is that doesn't mean
they're always going to do it

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:10.800
exactly justly or exactly fairly.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:13.735
But one other thing that I
do reference in this portion

00:14:13.735 --> 00:14:16.460
is that many of the things that Trump

00:14:17.454 --> 00:14:19.550
now says about immigration,
although he says them

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:22.490
perhaps without the great
cadence of Barrack Obama

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:25.070
are the exact same
things that Barack Obama

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:28.290
and Bill Clinton, and Nancy
Pelosi, and Diane Finstein,

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.330
and Chuck Schumer, and
all of them have said

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:31.200
in the past.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:33.680
So I think when you whittle
it down to the truth

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:36.720
most people actually
do believe in borders.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:39.750
Most people, look we know we
have something like 12 million

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:40.620
illegal immigrants here.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:42.156
I'm for pathway to citizenship.

00:14:42.156 --> 00:14:44.710
I don't want to kick all these people out.

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:46.390
But we have to start figuring out a way

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:47.530
to talk about this honestly.

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:50.170
It can't just be open
borders for everybody,

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:52.672
or everybody else is racist.

00:14:52.672 --> 00:14:54.800
- Yeah, I have a wall around my house

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:56.480
and I have a front door.

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:58.092
That's kind of the wall.

00:14:58.092 --> 00:15:01.042
- Yeah, isn't that something.

00:15:01.042 --> 00:15:03.820
- Yeah, and so the libertarian argument

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:05.920
that let the economy decide.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:07.830
So, we're in southern California,

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:09.510
a lot of Mexicans come here to work.

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:11.108
They do great work.

00:15:11.108 --> 00:15:12.530
They wouldn't come here

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:14.410
if there wasn't economic opportunity.

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:18.110
So why not just let the market determine.

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:20.930
Now, someone like a
think a Heather McDonald

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:24.100
or a more traditional
conservative would say,

00:15:24.100 --> 00:15:27.700
but they're not bringing
American value with them.

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:30.570
So even if they're hard
working we want them

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:32.547
to believe in the Constitution,

00:15:32.547 --> 00:15:36.490
the Civil Rights and things that America,

00:15:36.490 --> 00:15:40.372
that define Americanism
versus other traditions.

00:15:40.372 --> 00:15:43.530
That seems to be harder
to kind of articulate

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:46.000
or quantify in immigration policy.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:46.860
- Yes.

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:48.710
It's absolutely harder to quantify.

00:15:48.710 --> 00:15:50.320
I'm with them on the spirit of that.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:53.013
I don't want to insult any
of these individual people

00:15:53.013 --> 00:15:57.070
of course, but we don't know exactly,

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:59.930
if everyone's just coming here
for work that doesn't mean

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:01.670
that they share in the American ethos.

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:04.100
I also don't want to force
anyone to share in the

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:05.390
American ethos.

00:16:05.390 --> 00:16:07.510
So I think you're right that quantifying

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:09.900
actually is the hard part.

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.920
And that's why I say, you
know, you can lead this.

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:15.280
Every administration can set their policy.

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:17.360
This is one of the things
that the federal government

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:18.980
is supposed to do.

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:21.910
You know what, if you don't
like Trump's policy on it

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:23.320
then don't vote for Trump.

00:16:23.320 --> 00:16:26.610
Then if you get a Democrat
in that maybe has a more

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:29.140
liberal policy, a more open
policy and you don't like that

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:30.250
then vote against that guy.

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:32.450
That actually is the beauty of our system

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:35.240
because it's not that
we're setting a policy

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:38.160
and this is the policy
now for the next 20 years.

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:39.370
- Right.

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:41.719
Yeah, so it's always adjustable on that.

00:16:41.719 --> 00:16:44.930
Even if you can't
quantify it there is a way

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:47.410
to kind of articulate it and have some

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:49.386
kind of reasonable policy.

00:16:49.386 --> 00:16:52.113
You know, at the end of
the moral arch I sort of

00:16:52.113 --> 00:16:54.650
speculate about a future,
a world without borders.

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:58.150
In a way we were getting
there without economic borders

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:01.330
because of the internet, you
know, at least the borders

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:04.540
were very porous and it was
easy to trade with other people.

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:08.330
I can imagine that continuing
where the political unit

00:17:08.330 --> 00:17:10.863
is the city state, not the nation state.

00:17:10.863 --> 00:17:14.700
Unfortunately since I wrote
that in 2015 we've kind of gone

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:17.210
the other direction of
you know nation states

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:18.790
gaining more power.

00:17:18.790 --> 00:17:21.354
But I like the idea of local solutions.

00:17:21.354 --> 00:17:24.731
The pothole out here
on my street, you know,

00:17:24.731 --> 00:17:26.330
the federal government
I can't count on them.

00:17:26.330 --> 00:17:28.000
I just want the local Santa Barbara people

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:29.690
to come up and fix my pothole.

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:31.729
They're the one responsible.

00:17:31.729 --> 00:17:34.220
Kind of that localism.

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:36.588
- I make that same argument
literally about our messed

00:17:36.588 --> 00:17:40.040
up exit off the 405 near my house.

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:41.460
I mean that's absolutely true.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:42.890
By the way, one other
thing on this quickly.

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:44.751
Obviously I wrote this before coronavirus,

00:17:44.751 --> 00:17:47.130
but one of the interesting
things that's popping

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:49.850
up right now is that
suddenly you've got all

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:51.510
of these lefties that are saying oh,

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:54.040
we should have closed the
border to China a lot sooner,

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:56.270
even though Trump was
actually fairly ahead on that

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:58.500
and they were calling
him racist for doing it.

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:01.565
But you know, the truth of
the matter these are the

00:18:01.565 --> 00:18:02.580
same people that just
two weeks before that

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:05.940
would have been calling for
open borders and allowing

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:09.715
totally free back and forth all the time.

00:18:09.715 --> 00:18:11.311
So it's one of those things that I think,

00:18:11.311 --> 00:18:12.144
you know, you sort of said before.

00:18:12.144 --> 00:18:14.985
When the rubber hits the road I think

00:18:14.985 --> 00:18:17.130
people's policies start
making a little more sense

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:20.586
because they're important
and they impact them

00:18:20.586 --> 00:18:22.517
rather than just at the
idea level when you can

00:18:22.517 --> 00:18:23.815
just sort of say something

00:18:23.815 --> 00:18:24.648
and it doesn't necessarily matter.

00:18:24.648 --> 00:18:26.522
- Right, okay, abortion issue,

00:18:26.522 --> 00:18:27.837
again another contentious one.

00:18:27.837 --> 00:18:31.170
The Republicans have glommed
onto this as like one

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:34.368
of their key issues in the
last quarter centuries or so

00:18:34.368 --> 00:18:39.020
and, you know, you're
pro-choice largely and so am I.

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:43.180
So let's just say it is
a living human being.

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:44.900
It may not be a legal person yet,

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:46.750
depends where you draw the line.

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:48.590
You know if a woman's in her 3rd trimester

00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:50.590
and she's murdered it's a double homicide.

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:53.483
So the state does recognize
it as a person at some point.

00:18:54.350 --> 00:18:58.830
But isn't it reasonable
to argue that there is a,

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:00.610
not just a quantitative difference

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:03.663
but a qualitative difference
the moment of conception.

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:06.970
So, everybody agrees a
sperm and an egg that's

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:09.670
not a living human being,
you've got to have both.

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:11.380
But the moment conception happens,

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:16.380
even if it's just a 16 cell blastocyst

00:19:16.595 --> 00:19:20.290
and the moment a women gets
pregnant when she wants

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:24.410
to be pregnant she talk about
her baby as three weeks old.

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:26.650
My baby is six weeks old.

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:27.910
I can't wait to have my baby.

00:19:27.910 --> 00:19:30.760
They don't talk about it
like it's a piece of medical

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:32.980
tissue that has to be
removed like a tumor.

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:35.250
They talk about it like
it's a human being.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:37.789
So why is it not murder?

00:19:37.789 --> 00:19:40.670
- So, I love that you're
steelmanning this one

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:41.503
of all of them.

00:19:41.503 --> 00:19:43.880
And as you know that my
last line in this chapter

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.987
is now that you all hate me let's move on.

00:19:45.987 --> 00:19:48.650
So I make the concession.

00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:51.110
What you just did there
I make that concession.

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:53.800
Look, we can leave it to
ethicists and religious

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:56.490
leaders and scientists
to exactly tell us what

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:58.940
the moment when life really begins.

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:01.230
I will concede that when the sperm meets

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:04.390
the egg I'm willing to take
that as the beginning of life.

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:06.260
Now again, you can talk about
three days later and the

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:08.190
blastocyst and when it
attaches to the uterine wall.

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.700
And I happen to be fairly
well versed in this right now

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:12.390
because as you know David
and I have started the

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:15.010
process of having kids,
which for two guys ends

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:18.192
up being a lot more clinical
than maybe you'd want

00:20:18.192 --> 00:20:20.909
it to be, or than it is
for a heterosexual couple.

00:20:20.909 --> 00:20:25.240
But what my basic position
on this is is that

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:29.620
at 12 weeks, up to 12 weeks
whether you like it or not

00:20:29.620 --> 00:20:32.270
if you live in a pluralistic
society that is not

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:35.627
a religious theocracy you have to give

00:20:35.627 --> 00:20:37.550
that choice to the woman.

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:39.430
Now hopefully the man
who was involved too,

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:41.080
but at least to the woman.

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:42.250
I don't like it.

00:20:42.250 --> 00:20:43.600
I don't like the idea of it.

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:47.190
You know, I like the phrase
it should be rare and safe

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:48.630
is the idea.

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:50.910
But then there's a lot of cascading issues

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:52.990
for the purely right to life crowd.

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:55.610
I mean first off I chatted
with a lot of right to lifers

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:59.020
who will tell me privately
that although they take that

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:02.920
hardcore no compromise
position that privately

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:05.670
they know if their daughter
got raped or if they

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:08.270
found out if their
daughter had a child with

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:10.700
severe brain abnormalities
that could never

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:14.690
live a fully actualized life
that they would do something.

00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:16.800
Now, that's not a reason
to make abortion legal.

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:19.330
But I think it shows that
we all live with certain

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:22.560
inequities and inconsistencies
in our ideology.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:24.460
So I think the best you can do,

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:26.770
again, if you're just
trying to create guardrails

00:21:26.770 --> 00:21:29.720
on a society that's gonna
allow people from all

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:32.480
walks of life and religious
beliefs and philosophical

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:33.620
beliefs and everything else,

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:36.280
if you're just trying to
guard as many of those people

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:40.751
to be free then you pick
a fairly early time in the

00:21:40.751 --> 00:21:42.150
pregnancy, which is first trimester,

00:21:42.150 --> 00:21:45.160
and then you put a couple
caveats around that related

00:21:45.160 --> 00:21:46.610
to the mother's health,

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:48.600
and then, you know, severe abnormalities

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:50.490
that could show up beyond that.

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:54.898
But I just, it's unfortunate,
and you know abortion

00:21:54.898 --> 00:21:56.650
unfortunately has been
fetishized in a weird way

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:59.480
by some on the left and
all of those things.

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:01.020
This is the hardest ones to talk about

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:03.900
and I don't think that there's
an absolutely great answer.

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:05.070
I will just say one other thing.

00:22:05.070 --> 00:22:08.460
I know we see largely similarly on this.

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:11.940
That I think in many ways
this is the one that keeps

00:22:11.940 --> 00:22:14.860
let's say people like you from
saying you're a conservative,

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:17.910
and keeps conservatives from
saying people like Shermer

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:19.425
is a conservative, that abortion,

00:22:19.425 --> 00:22:23.020
I've noticed this with a
lot of my sort of liberally

00:22:23.020 --> 00:22:26.820
libertarian friends or
let's say ex lefties

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:29.240
that this is the one that
they go, I could never be

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:30.073
one of them.

00:22:30.073 --> 00:22:31.560
And this is the one
where the conservatives

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:34.240
are like yeah, you can
kind of never be one of us.

